  "problem": "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Diana's plot of land is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nmeters.",
  "worked_solution": "The width of the plot is $x-1$ meters, and its area is $2{x}^{2}-2x$ square meters. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(2{x}^{2},-2x)=2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2x & = \\\\ (2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot (-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot (-1)) & = \\\\ 2x(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $2x$ meters.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184436/assets/dadb8090017f300275d803d488d401435b4f0e29b26ddf19e5542dba60a6bec4.png)",
